satellite images show a buildup of  russian military activity at the  ukrainian border in recent weeks there  is concern that russia is actually  getting ready to invade its smaller  neighbor the kremlin denies this  but u.s intelligence revealed that  moscow has drawn up plans for a military  invasion of ukraine that could involve  around 175 000 troops as soon as next  year the meeting with putin i was very  straightforward if in fact  he invades ukraine there will be severe  consequences  ukraine has been trying to join western  organizations like nato and the eu but  this is at odds with what russia wants  which is to have influence over the  country  the u.s and russia have recently  expelled diplomats over cyber crime and  clashed over energy supplies and a  migrant crisis on the border between  belarus and poland after the summit  between president biden and president  putin in june there was a glimmer of  hope that the countries might set a  different course but again there has  been this struggle  here's how the clash between the us and  russia over ukraine is worsening an  already tense relationship  russia invaded ukraine and seized the  ukrainian peninsula of crimea after the  country staged a pro-western revolution  in 2014.  ever since then russian president  vladimir putin has tried to prevent  ukraine's drift towards the west ukraine  has for a long time wanted to join nato  and putin has voiced anger over the us  and its allies financial and military  support of the country  the kremlin has made very clear that  they feel that any kind of deployment of  nato troops in eastern ukraine which  would be russia's western borders  would be a threat and for russia that is  a red line  the key question would be especially in  relation to the russian buildup of  troops on the border  how far will the u.s and nato be willing  to go in order to protect ukraine  while the u.s and nato have no  obligation to help ukraine the biden  administration has threatened serious  sanctions against russia and said it  wouldn't tolerate another invasion like  the one in 2014  washington has also said it would help  protect ukraine's sovereignty but press  kiev to reform internal issues like  corruption  tensions between russia and the u.s have  been building for a while  the us recently imposed sanctions and  ordered the expulsion of more than 50  russian diplomats by june 30th based on  allegations of cyber attacks and u.s  election interference  moscow retaliated by doing the same  ordering the removal of several u.s  embassy personnel from russia by next  year this is a kind of tit for tat what  they call mirror measures taken against  the u.s who they say has basically  violated diplomatic norms so there is  definitely this this back and forth this  diplomatic tug of war if you wish in a  call with putin in december biden warned  that if russia made any moves in ukraine  the us would prevent the completion of  russia's nordstream ii pipeline which  washington sees as a threat to european  energy independence  the pipeline would transport natural gas  from russia to europe and be a huge  source of energy revenue for moscow  russia is a major exporter of gas to  europe and an important channel runs  through belarus  belarusian president alexander  lukashenko recently threatened to  disrupt that flow of energy after the eu  imposed sanctions on the country over a  migrant crisis in november on its border  with poland  western leaders have accused russia of  helping exacerbate the crisis a claim  that moscow denies  earlier on in biden's presidency  washington had wanted to establish a  closer relationship with kremlin  but as tensions escalate over ukraine  political analysts say the two aren't  making much progress towards that goal a  lot of it can boil down to the fact that  you know russia feels that  it has been ignored both countries have  said of course we do not want this  confrontation  but it seems as though a lot of the  times it's one step forward two steps  back as they each try to maintain  influence and not want to be seen as the  weaker between the two sides
 

